<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Begteginid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1144 CE – 1233 CE (approx.) · capital Erbil</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkmen dynasty of atabegs at Erbil whose ruler Gökböri, Saladin's brother-in-law, made the city famous for celebrating the Prophet's birthday.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayn-al-din-ali-kuchuk">Zayn al-Din Ali Kuchuk</a>, son of Begtegin, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> officer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a> who became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> and other towns around 1144. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zengid-dynasty-of-mosul">Zengid dynasty of Mosul</a> he built up a personal domain in the plains east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, which his descendants inherited.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia-khatun">Rabi'a Khatun</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187 and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> as an Ayyubid ally in 1190. He ruled until 1233, building hospitals, hostels for travellers, schools and orphanages, and staging lavish public celebrations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawlid">Mawlid</a>, the Prophet's birthday, among the earliest recorded. The biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khallikan">Ibn Khallikan</a>, a native of Erbil, praised his generosity.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori">Gökböri</a> died without a male heir in 1233 and bequeathed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> to the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustansir-i">al-Mustansir</a>, who annexed it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took the city in 1258.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1144 CE – 1233 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.47%;width:1.77%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1144 CE – 1233 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Erbil</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali Kuchuk, Gökböri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Begteginids, Atabegs of Erbil</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1188&amp;focus=begteginid-dynasty" aria-label="Begteginid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5788 872 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Begteginid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6108 1128L6085 1123M6108 1128L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6085 1123h0M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6108 1128h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6108 1128h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Erbil. <a href="/atlas?year=1188&amp;focus=begteginid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
